{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261028T171401Z-c8c650a5", "ts": "2026-10-28T17:16:32Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.2", "branch": "fix/second-clone-evidence", "build": "3026cacc8d89"}, "body": {"base_commit": "3026cacc8d894688704ad7bc616a4274212f4bc9", "base_tree": "5a3db064e225522a95a1b8927d26d9bbeaacaaae", "head_tree": "64798ed2293a7410195ea6eff288f6e907472ec8", "head_commit": "8df9a4eba90ea2fd607a3832ffbcb1c222eb64da", "dispatch_commit": "8df9a4eba90ea2fd607a3832ffbcb1c222eb64da", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "tests/test_critic_mode_inference.py", "tests/test_critic_session_guard.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-second-clone-evidence.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.8.0.md", ".prawduct/changes/second-clone-evidence.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "tests/test_critic_mode_inference.py", "tests/test_critic_session_guard.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-second-clone-evidence.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.8.0.md", ".prawduct/changes/second-clone-evidence.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "warning", "title": "A covered clean branch falls through to rule-4 `chunk`, which recommends a review that has nothing to review or the whole branch", "recommendation": "When `_branch_already_covered` declines the cumulative in rule 2 / rule 4's redirect (not mid-plan), inference now falls through to `return \"chunk\", \"rule-4 chunk: ... no cumulative precondition\"` (critic_mode.py ~L405-414). The rule-4 comment just above says a mode that cannot review anything is not the answer to 'what should I run'. For `covered`, `gates.covered_frontier` is HEAD, so the chunk interval is empty and critic-begin answers 'no review needed' only after a round-trip. For `transferred` (base advanced), covered_frontier returns None ('no tree on the walk composes', its docstring), so `working_tree_interval_base` falls to the merge-base on a clean tree, and the recommended `chunk` re-reviews the whole branch at inner rigor, which the gate does not need either. Answer `deferred` (or an explicit no-review-owed answer) with a rationale that names the gate's status, as `_mid_plan_answer` already does for NOTHING_UNREVIEWED. Pin the returned mode in `TestRule2AsksThePrGate::test_silent_when_composed_coverage_passes_and_no_cache_exists`, which only asserts `!= \"cumulative\"`, and add a transferred-status case.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "tests/test_critic_mode_inference.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Cache derivation reads unvalidated note envelopes as facts (schema-ahead included), and its guard catches only OSError", "recommendation": "`ingest` passes `[*known['facts'], *arrived]` to `derive_missing_cache`. `arrived` holds raw envelopes from `_parse`, which keeps a schema-ahead record of any kind, and `append_envelope` checks only the envelope. So a newer plugin's `review` record, which `read_facts` would put in `schema_ahead`, can be picked as the newest branch review and rendered by `fact_to_cache_record`, a projection that assumes this reader's body shape. That cache is then never overwritten. A body that does not match that shape (e.g. `findings: null`, a non-dict `counts`/roster entry) raises TypeError/AttributeError, and `_derive_cache` catches only OSError. The exception then escapes `ingest` after the appends have landed. `evidence ingest` and `evidence sync` traceback, and session start's broad except reports 'ingest failed' with appended=0 although facts were appended. That contradicts `_derive_cache`'s own 'Advice: a failure ... ingest stays ok'. Filter `arrived` to `evidence.SUPPORTED_SCHEMAS` (or derive from a fresh `read_facts`), widen the guard to Exception per the advice contract, and add a test that ingests a schema-ahead review and a malformed review body into a cache-less clone.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Two of the three surfaces that announce a derived cache, and the carried-blocker branch, are untested", "recommendation": "Only the `evidence ingest` CLI line is tested. Session start's `cache_line` print (prawduct-hook `_session_start_ingest`) and `evidence sync`'s `notes.append(cache_line(...))` are not, though the change fragment names all three. `derive_missing_cache`'s verify-anchor path, which carries the anchor's unresolved blockers, is not asserted with a non-empty `carried` either. Add one assertion per surface, plus a derived-from-verify case where the anchor keeps an unresolved blocker, and check that it reaches `next_action`.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/evidence_sync.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "A review anchored at the merge base can be a sibling branch's, and becomes this clone's cache when nothing further along exists", "recommendation": "`_branch_commits` includes the merge base itself, because a branch's first dirty-tree review anchors there. A sibling branch cut from the same base anchors its first dirty-tree review on that same commit. When the only branch-range review in the store is that sibling's, `derive_missing_cache` makes it the cache. `_prior_review_fact`'s ancestor check passes, since the merge base is an ancestor, so rule 1 and verify-resolutions in this clone then anchor on the sibling's findings. Consider preferring a review whose `actor.branch` matches the checked-out branch when the anchor is the merge base, or skip merge-base-only anchors for derivation.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "derive_missing_cache decides whether to derive by 'the file exists', which cannot tell a stale or off-branch cache from this branch's own consolidation", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: whether the single-slot cache describes this branch's newest review is decided by `findings_path.exists()`, a stand-in for state the file already records (`fact_id`, the anchor commit). The docstring justifies the predicate with 'an existing cache is this clone's own consolidation (or a later one)', and that is false in two reachable cases. (a) The clone has a cache from another branch. Every working clone that ever ran a review has one, because the cache survives a branch switch. Derivation is then skipped, `_prior_review_fact` refuses the off-lineage anchor as not-an-ancestor, and verify-resolutions still cannot run on a branch reviewed in the other clone. That is the #1057 symptom, in exactly the second-committer clone the consumer note promises it works in. (b) Clone B derived its cache from review R1, and clone A later ingests review R2 further along the branch. B's cache stays on R1, so B's verify dispatch anchors to the superseded review. Fix: derive when there is no cache, OR when the existing cache's anchor is not in `branch_commits`, OR when it is behind the newest on-branch review and was itself ingest-derived (record that provenance in the written record). Keep refusing to replace a consolidation-written cache that is on this branch and not behind. Add a test for each case. If the narrower scope is intended, narrow the consumer note and the docstring to 'a clone with no findings cache', and file the rest.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Rule 4 and explicit-args docs and the rule-4 chunk rationale don't name the new 'branch already covered' exception", "recommendation": "Instance; prose, so NOTE. Several texts still state the old condition. The module docstring's rule 4 and the rule-4 block comment ('a clean tree with a dispatchable bundle answers `cumulative`') do. So does `_explicit_mode`'s 'Only where the redirect works' list, which enumerates when the token stands and omits composed coverage. On a covered clean branch, inference now falls through to `rule-4 chunk: ... no cumulative precondition` (or 'no other rule fired'). The rationale gives no sign that the coverage check suppressed the cumulative, and the result is the empty-interval answer the rule-4 comment says inference avoids. Either add the covered exception to those sentences, or have rule 4 return a rationale that names the covered verdict. No other edit is wanted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Transport module now reaches up into consolidation (import cycle) to write a Critic view", "recommendation": "Instance; recorded design choice (plan Decisions: one owner where facts arrive). Two things follow from it. `evidence_notes.ingest` now lazily imports `critic_consolidate`, which imports `evidence_notes` at module top. And the notes-transport layer carries Critic-specific wording (`cache_line`). The single owner is a sound reason. If the layering matters later, a small post-ingest hook owned on the Critic side would invert the dependency. No change is required now.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "'Never overwrite' rationale does not cover a cache ingest itself derived, so the second clone pins the first review it ingests", "recommendation": "derive_missing_cache explains never-overwrite this way: 'an existing cache is this clone's own consolidation (or a later one)'. Since this change, ingest is a second writer of that slot, so the premise no longer holds. Clone B ingests clone A's review R1 and gets a cache derived from R1. Clone A then runs R2 (say a cumulative with blockers) and B ingests it, but B's cache still says R1, because the derived cache now 'exists'. B's verify-resolutions then anchors on R1 through _prior_review_fact's fact_id. R1 is still an ancestor, so nothing refuses, and the pass resolves the wrong review's findings. The same premise fails for a cache left by this clone's own review on another branch: the single slot survives a branch switch, nothing is derived, and verify refuses with 'not an ancestor'. That contradicts the change fragment's consumer note ('verify-resolutions works on fixes to it'). The rule this departs from is hook-surface.md's 'Denormalized/derived fields maintained by independent actors drift \u2014 compute them on demand from the source of truth, or validate them mechanically after writes'. Scope: class (every case where the slot holds something other than this lineage's newest review). Fix it in derive_missing_cache, the one owner. Re-derive when the existing cache is not this clone's consolidation of a review at or past the newest on-branch one: its fact_id is off this branch's lineage, or it names a review older than the one just selected. Alternatively, mark a derived cache so a later ingest may replace it. Then add a two-ingest test (R1 then R2) and an off-lineage-cache test. If you keep the narrower behaviour, record that decision in the plan and scope the consumer note to clones with no findings cache.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "Cache derivation fails silently and swallows only OSError, so failures look like 'nothing to derive' or are misreported", "recommendation": "_derive_cache's docstring promises that 'a failure leaves the clone as it was \u2026 reported as None'. It catches only OSError. Any other exception raised inside derive_missing_cache, fact_to_cache_record or carried_blocking (for example AttributeError on a malformed ingested body; _is_envelope checks only that body is a dict) escapes after the store append has already happened. `evidence ingest` and `evidence sync` then end in a traceback with the records actually appended. Session start's broad except turns it into 'evidence ingest at session start failed \u2026 the briefing reads this clone's store without the other clones' notes', which is false because the notes were appended. On the other side, the paths that do fail (an OSError, or a written record that gates.validate_critic_findings rejects and that gets unlinked) return None. That is indistinguishable from 'no on-branch review' or 'cache already present', so the only thing the operator sees is verify-resolutions refusing later with 'no readable prior findings cache'. Per hook-surface.md, 'Pick each fallback's direction from the invariant it protects: \u2026 degraded advice names its consequence, and a swallow is fixed in the frame that discards it'. Scope: instance. Catch broadly in _derive_cache, which is the advice boundary, and return a reason alongside None (for example result['cache_reason']) so cache_line or the ingest reason can say 'findings cache not derived (<why>); verify-resolutions cannot anchor here'. Add a test that forces a non-OSError and asserts ingest stays ok with appended > 0.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions and what settled them", "recommendation": "Scope: none. For the owner: (1) Re-derive the cache at ingest rather than teach each reader a store fallback. Settled in the plan's Decisions, because the verify reviewer reads the file and cannot fall back. (2) Use branch position, not ts or store order, to pick the 'newest' review. Settled in the derive_missing_cache docstring and the fragment (ts is to the second; ingest appends in note-read order). (3) Commit 8df9a4eb split 'already covered' out of _fresh_cumulative_covers_head into _branch_already_covered, asked only where a cumulative would be named, because folding it in silenced rule 4 before _mid_plan_answer. This was a mid-build correction found by test and recorded in the commit message and the docstring. (4) data-model's derived-view norm: dispositioned 'conforms' with ingest as a second, advisory writer. The architecture.md and api-contract.md dispositions are missing; record_lint reports them (Goal 2). No alignment-pass miss: the plan records 'Response taken: Proceed' on the owner's 2026-10-28 request.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-second-clone-evidence.md"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: #1057 governance: verify-resolutions in a second clone finds no prior review", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The change fragment already declares closes: [brookstalley/prawduct#1057]. Verify it (note that the derived-cache pinning WARNING above narrows when it holds), then call `/prawduct:backlog update 1057 status=shipped closed-by=scope:second-clone-evidence` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says to. Checked and not resolved by this change: the related open items #1058 (stats double-count ingested reviews), #1115 (cross-clone stats roll-up), #890 (bound repeat cumulatives), #672 (coverage composes by tree but gates key on identity) and #928. Backlog cache confirmed 5h old.", "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings cross-check and Records Pass coverage", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Learnings read via learnings-files --for-diff: core.md, authoring.md, gates.md, hook-surface.md, release.md, reviews.md, tests.md. Escalations: hook-surface.md's derived-fields rule and its fallback-direction rule are quoted in the two WARNINGs above. This cycle added or changed no learnings rules, and I saw no implicit supersession. Records Pass over files_oracle (api-contract.md, build-plan-second-clone-evidence.md, release-plan-v3.8.0.md, changes/second-clone-evidence.md): the api-contract ingest and status wording matches the code (the `cache` key in --json, the status range suffix), and the release-plan row is correct. The only claim that clears a bar is the change fragment's consumer note ('verify-resolutions works on fixes to it' in a second clone). It ships, and it overstates for any clone that already holds a findings cache. It is folded into the derived-cache WARNING rather than raised separately, because fixing that code makes the note true.", "fid": "R-12"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 5, "note": 7}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "second-clone-evidence", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01"], "chunks_proposed": ["01"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "3026cacc8d894688704ad7bc616a4274212f4bc9", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".pra